The '''Cantonal''' '''sovereignty amendment''' ({{Langx|bl|Esmena de Sobirania Cantonal}}), popularly known as the '''Cantonal Veto''', is a proposed amendment to the [[Constitution of Balisca]]. First proposed via a [[W:Citizens' initiative|citizens' initiative]] in June 2024, the amendment would empower the cantons with the collective ability to block a [[confederal emergency]] declaration.

After a fifteen-month signature-gathering campaign, the initiative was certified by the [[Confederal Electoral Commission]] on 25 September 2025, having surpassed the required threshold of signatures from 5% of the electorate. The proposal has now advanced to the public deliberation phase on the [[Open Cabildo]].

The proposed amendment represents one of the most significant and polarizing constitutional debates in Balisca.

== History and background ==
The concept of a cantonal veto has deep roots in Baliscan politics dating back all the way to the [[Articles of Confederation]] and the [[Confederal Question]]. The debate was reignited in the late 1970s, growing out of fears of [[Confederal government of Balisca|confederal government]] overreach following the confederal emergencies of 1976, and the increasing authoritarian rhetoric and actions of [[Mário Sá Justino]] and the [[Revolutionary Front]]. While a previous version of the amendment failed in the 1980s, the idea was revived in the 2020s in response to new political tensions.

The modern push for the amendment began with the [[Diego Navarro|Navarro administration]]'s arguments that the worsening [[W:Climate crisis|climate crisis]] constituted a confederal emergency. This prompted fears among autonomist platforms that a [[W:Climate emergency declaration|climate emergency]] could be used to indefinitely centralize power and override cantonal sovereignty. A broad coalition of autonomist groups, local communes, and civil liberties advocates organized the citizens' initiative, launching their grassroots campaign during the [[2021 Baliscan civil unrest]] to build support for an amendment to create a constitutional "firewall" against perceived confederal overreach.

